  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 230 PM CDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nThe trough in the east sees some reorientation this week. The\ninitial trough lifts northeast out of New England and into the\nCanadian Maritimes while another trough arrives from the west\nand digs into the Great Lakes to replace it. Meanwhile, the\nSouthwest Ridge remains largely in the same location with some\ndaily wobbles. For us locally we'll maintain the persistent\nnorthwest flow aloft with richer moisture remaining suppressed \nto the south and west. Temperatures continue to gradually \nrebound through the mid and even upper 80s by Wednesday.\n\nThe next trough arriving in the Great Lakes pushes a cold front\nsouthward through our forecast area Wednesday into Wednesday \nnight. This represents our next best chance for rain, but those \nchances remain fairly limited locally. The better forcing with \nthe trough passes by to our north, while the richer moisture \nremains suppressed to our south. The low resolution ensemble \n(LREF) guidance remains more ambitious with rain chances than \nthe NBM. Of the LREF guidance, 60 to 70 percent of members \nproduce rain across our forecast area in the 24 hours ending \nWednesday night, up 10 percent from yesterday. Meanwhile, NBM \n12hour PoP remains in the 10 to 20 percent range. This \ndiscrepancy is partially a result of timing variance in the \nguidance, but also uncertainty in the development of another \nconvective complex to our west over the Plains linking up with \nthe better moisture. The LREF may also be overestimating the \nrain chances a bit due to the lower resolution of this guidance,\nsomething NBM is tuned to adjust for with low QPF events. If we\nlook deeper in the LREF we see the percentage of members that\nproduce 0.05 inch or greater rainfall is only 30 to 40 percent \nover that same time period. This gives credence to the idea that\nthe low resolution guidance may be indicating more widespread \nlight rain amounts than should be expected as precipitation in \nthis time period should be convective in nature.\n\nThe air mass behind Wednesday's front is not as cool and dry as\nthe last one, and we only get a small taste of it. So we do see\nthe high temperature forecast for Thursday and Friday a few\ndegrees lower than Wednesday, but still near normal for this\ntime of year, in the mid 80s. Guidance is in good agreement that\nthe western ridge flattens and spreads out across the southern\ntier of the country this weekend into the following week. This\nreduces the extremity of the heat within the ridge, but also\nbroadens that warmth back into our area. By Saturday the NBM\nprobability of 90 degrees creeps back up into the 20 to 40\npercent range. Mercifully, the humidity remains limited at least\ninitially, with NBM probability of 70 dewpoint only up to about\n40 percent by the same time frame.",
